                  <msItem>
                     <title>Excerpta ex decretis</title>
                     <note type="MLGB3_generalNotes">Probably an Exeter scribe. The title at the
                        verso of the fourth flyleaf, opposite first leaf of text, has (s. xii):
                        'Exceptiones ex decretis romanorum pontificum. Epistole clementis pape ad
                        iacobum ierosolimonum episcopum'. To this Grandison adds 'Deincesp aliorum
                        summorum Pontificum decreta. Vel Epistole. vsque ad beatum Gregorium
                        primum'.</note>
                     <note type="MLGB3_generalNotes">31-32 long lines.</note>
                     <note type="MLGB3_generalNotes">Medieval binding of whit leather over boards
                        with chemise over them. On back cover of the chemise, there are marks of a
                        large label now lost which was secured by eight nails, and, at foot, a
                        complete chain attachment secured by four nails. Two strap and pin
                        attachments now missing.</note>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="11XX">s. xii</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_1">Exeter, Devon. Cathedral
                        church of St Peter. </orgName><p>At head of fol. 1, '. . . Liber Ecclesie
                        Cathedralis Exoniens'', in the hand of Bishop John Grandison (d.
                        1369).</p><p>On verso of fourth flyleaf, below the title, 'Hunc librum
                        legauit magister Walterus Gybbys ecclesie Cathedralis Exon' Canonicus dum
                        vixit in testamento suo ad vsum eiusdem ecclesie ibidem perpetuo
                        reman[surum] quem quidem librum execut' dicti defuncti deliber[ . . . .]
                        ecclesie Cathedralis predict' [. . .] mense Septembris [. . .] domini
                        millesimo CCCCmo XIIIIo.'</p><note type="MLGB3_medievalCatalogueNotes">"Cat.
                        pp. 304, 369"</note></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Given (or restored to the Cathedral) by
                     Walter Gybbys, canon (d. 1414), who also bequeathed Oxford, Bodleian, Bodley
                     810 (q.v.).</provenance>
               </history>
